# Your environment: ccic container `{{SUFFIX}}`

You are running inside a Docker container that belongs to you alone. The human
runs their own copy of this project directly on their machine (macOS). **The two
environments are deliberately kept apart** — you never share a port, a database,
or a compiled dependency with them. Nothing you start here can collide with
anything they have running.

## Showing the human something visual

Write images to **`{{SCREENSHOTS}}/`**. That directory is on the bind mount, so a
file you write there opens immediately on their machine.

This is the *only* channel for visual feedback — no port is published, so they
cannot open a dev server you start. If you need them to see rendering, take a
screenshot and tell them the path, relative to the project root:

```
{{SCREENSHOTS}}/checkout-form-overflow.png
```

Name files for what they show, not `screenshot-1.png`. You can read your own
screenshots back to check your work.

## What is installed

- **Playwright + Chromium**, globally, at `$PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH`. Headless
  only. Fonts and colour emoji are installed, so rendered text in screenshots is
  real text rather than boxes. This is ccic's Playwright, for your own headless
  checks — it is not the project's test dependency.
- **Node {{NODE_MAJOR}}**, installed outside mise, which is what Claude Code and
  Playwright run on. A project pinning a different node version in `mise.toml`
  will not disturb it.
- **mise**, with this project's `[tools]` installed: {{MISE_TOOLS}}
- `git`, `ripgrep`, `fd`, `jq`, `psql`, plus a normal build toolchain
  (`build-essential`, `pkg-config`, `libvips`, `libpq-dev`, `libyaml-dev`).

Start the app yourself if you need to drive it — that is what the toolchain is
here for.

